# Step-by-step implementation instructions (agent chat)

## When this applies

Use this format in **Agent chat** when the user will write the code themselves, e.g.:

- instructions / how to implement X / walk me through
- "what do I put in these functions" / "explain more in detail what needs to be done"
- pointing at a `.plan.md` slice and asking what to do next **in chat**
- a conversational "plan / checklist of what to do" when they are coding along (not authoring a plan doc)

## When this does NOT apply

- **Plan Mode or authoring/updating `*.plan.md`** — those stay high-level: approach, architecture, todos, security notes. Trigger on the mode/artifact, not the word "plan."
- Code review / bug findings — use the cite-code-locations rule instead.
- Actual implementation when the user asked you to write the code — implement; don't only lecture.

## Default vs practice mode

**Default:** full pasteable sketches (signatures + bodies with real syntax).

**Practice mode** — turn on when the user says e.g. "hints first", "let me try", or "practice mode":

- Still give **Where**, **Why**, **New concept**, **Mirror**, signature, the 1–2 APIs/symbols they'll need, and a way to test.
- Do **not** give the full function body until they say "show me" or paste their attempt.
- Practice-mode blanks / hints are intentional; they do **not** violate the comment-only-sketch ban (that ban still applies to default mode and to any full sketch you later reveal).

## Required format (match the “right” style)

Teach like a tutor for someone coding along. Structure:

1. **Short opener** — what’s already done vs what they still need (one or two sentences). No long “problem essay.”
2. **Scope** — **≤3** **What to write** blocks → one reply. **≥4** → use **Long guides (multi-turn)** below.
3. **Per piece / step** (only for the piece(s) in *this* reply):
   - Plain-language job in one short paragraph
   - **Why** — one or two lines on the design reason (e.g. “lock the row so two webhooks can’t credit twice”)
   - **New concept** — the one idea to take away (e.g. `with_for_update()` or idempotency via a unique column)
   - **Mirror** — existing code to copy the pattern from, with path + line range (e.g. `get_balance` in `Backend/Wallet/router.py` ~50)
   - **Where** — exact file path + class/function (or “new function under X”) + landmark + action
   - **What to write** — default: real code the reader can type or paste. Practice mode: signature + hints only (see above). Not a full file dump unless asked. Never replace a sketch with a numbered prose “flow.”
   - **Line / Meaning** table — required whenever any line in the snippet is not obvious (what that line does / why it’s there). Optional Part/Job or URL/Why tables only as extras.
4. Nested steps when one piece has phases (`### Step B1`, `B2`, …) — each step is something they can type next. If one function is large, **split into more steps or more turns** (helpers first, then the orchestrator), each with its own **What to write**.
5. **Does / does not** table when roles are easy to mix up (e.g. which model owns which column).
6. **Order in the file** — numbered list of where each block in *this* reply sits.
7. **Checkpoint** — every turn must include all of:
   - a check they can run (pytest command, HTTP call, or “imports clean”) and the expected result
   - one short **predict** question (e.g. “what happens if the same `tx_signature` arrives twice?”) — ties to quiz-me when they want a full quiz
   - “Paste your version and I’ll review it” (hand off to cite-code-locations on review)
   - what to ask for next (Piece N / Step Cn), or stop if this was the last piece

## Long guides (multi-turn)

When a plan slice or ask would need **≥4** **What to write** blocks, **do not** put every sketch in one reply. Combine:

1. **Menu** — short map of pieces only (title + one-line job each). No architecture essay; no sketch-free “how it works” checklist pretending to be the guide.
2. **One piece per reply** (default), or **one nested step per reply** if that piece alone is large (e.g. C1 helpers, then C2 orchestrator).
3. **Full tutor format for the current slice only** — Why + New concept + Mirror + Where + What to write + Line/Meaning. Later pieces stay names on the menu until the user asks for them.
4. **Checkpoint** — as above; do not pre-dump N+1’s sketches “for later.”

First reply pattern: menu + either (a) the first unfinished piece with full sketches (or practice hints), or (b) ask which piece they want if order is flexible.

Follow-up replies: skip re-explaining the whole menu; one sentence of context → current piece → next checkpoint.

| Do | Do not |
|---|---|
| Menu + current piece sketches | One mega-reply with every piece’s code |
| Split across turns when long | Replace sketches with a prose “flow to implement” to save space |
| Checkpoint, wait for “next” | Sketch-free overview of all pieces as the only answer |

## Location precision (mandatory)

Every edit step MUST state all of:

| Field | Example |
|---|---|
| File | `Backend/Wallet/models.py` |
| Target | class `CreditOrder`, column `quote_id` |
| Landmark | “below `paid_tx_hash`, lines ~100–105 today” |
| Action | change / add / delete / replace |

Never say only “change the order link” or show a snippet under the wrong class heading. If two similar names exist (`PaymentQuote` vs `CreditOrder`), say which one explicitly and use a **Does / does not** table.

Every piece needs a **Mirror** line (existing symbol + path + approx lines). For **new** endpoints or helpers also say: create vs edit, full route path (including router `prefix`), and function name.

## One recommended approach (no hedging)

Do **not** write “put this on WalletService (or a small `quotes.py`)…”.

- Prefer **one** concrete place that matches this repo’s patterns.
- If alternatives matter, use a short table: Approach / When / **Recommend**, then write all **What to write** steps for the recommended one only.

## Prerequisites and “same transaction”

Do **not** assume the reader knows a prior object exists.

- If a step needs an HTTP route, service method, or schema that does not exist yet, either (a) include a prior step that creates it, or (b) label the step **Later / blocked on …** and stop — do not pretend they can paste into a void.
- If you say “inside the same DB transaction…”, define it in that step: e.g. “one request handler / one service method; do `consume_quote` and `session.add(CreditOrder(...))` before the session commits (FastAPI `get_session` commits at end of request).” Point at existing commit/session pattern in this repo when possible.
- Placeholder payloads (`payload={...}`) must say what keys to put **now**, or mark pricing as a later piece with a minimal stub dict.

## Hard bans (the “wrong” style)

Do **not** answer with:

- A long **“The problem”** / architecture narrative before actionable steps
- Ownership/rationale essays as the **main body** (the short **Why** / **New concept** lines above are required; essays are not)
- High-level “conceptually (not implementing)” bullet lists with no sketches (or no practice-mode hints)
- Plan-doc tone: summary sections, “in short” restating the whole answer, optional deep dives instead of coding steps
- Vague checklist only (“register callback, then bootstrap, then keep getMe thin”)
- Ambiguous target (“change X”) without file + class/symbol
- Hedging between files/modules without a single recommendation
- Referring to unbuilt endpoints/transactions as if they already exist
- **“Flow to implement” / numbered prose algorithm** in place of **What to write**
- **Comment-only sketches** in default mode (narrating RPC/DB steps in `# comments` is not code the reader can type)
- **Skipping or thinning What to write because the function is long or “obvious.”** Length → Step C1/C2/C3, never a summary

Brief context (a few lines + one existing-code cite) is fine **only** to set up the next step — then immediately go to Piece/Step.

## Pre-send check (mandatory)

Before sending, each edit step must have:

1. **Where** with all four fields: file, target, landmark, action
2. **Why**, **New concept**, and **Mirror**
3. **What to write** — default: real syntax (defs, assigns, awaits), not only bullets or `# comments`; practice mode: signature + hints is enough until they ask for the body
4. **Line / Meaning** if any line isn’t obvious
5. **Checkpoint** with run-check + expected result, one predict question, and “paste your version”

If any item fails, rewrite that step before sending. Do not “compress for readability” by dropping sketches (or practice hints).

